WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.165
Many AI systems depend on data pipelines before inference ever happens.

00:00:04.165 --> 00:00:07.610
A model-serving issue may actually begin with a failed ingestion job,

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:10.810
a delayed index update, stale content, missing embeddings,

00:00:10.810 --> 00:00:12.930
or a retrieval source that is unavailable.

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:17.115
Data and retrieval telemetry helps teams avoid treating every output problem as a model

00:00:17.115 --> 00:00:18.000
problem.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.540
Retrieval-augmented systems need evidence about ingestion status, document processing,

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:26.860
indexing success, embedding job progress, vector database health, retrieval latency,

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:28.280
and result availability.

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:31.235
Teams also need to know which index, corpus, data source,

00:00:31.235 --> 00:00:33.780
or content version participated in a request.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:37.960
That context makes it possible to connect user-facing behavior to the underlying knowledge

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:38.740
source.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.360
Freshness and lifecycle status matter.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:44.310
If content is stale, deprecated, partially indexed, or waiting on approval,

00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:48.140
the system may return less reliable results while all infrastructure dashboards look

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:48.645
healthy.

00:00:48.645 --> 00:00:51.525
Telemetry should surface when data moved, when it was validated,

00:00:51.525 --> 00:00:55.460
when it became available for retrieval, and when it should no longer be used.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:57.720
Permission-aware retrieval adds another requirement.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:00.180
Teams need evidence that access decisions were made,

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:04.220
but logs should not expose protected document contents or sensitive source material.

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:09.510
Batch jobs and streaming pipelines also need failure counts, retry behavior, backlog size,

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:12.250
processing latency, and data quality indicators.

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:16.665
Pipeline telemetry helps teams separate model behavior from data availability, indexing,

00:01:16.665 --> 00:01:17.960
retrieval, and governance issues.
